1. Requirements
- Docker Desktop
- Node.js and npm
- Visual Studio Code or another text editor
- Windows PowerShell
Start Docker Desktop and verify it:
docker --version
docker info


2. Create the project
mkdir ecommerce
cd ecommerce
npm init -y
npm install express


This creates the Node.js project and installs Express.
3. Configure package.json
Open package.json and make sure the scripts section contains:
"scripts": {
  "start": "node server.js"
}


Keep the existing dependencies section generated by npm.
4. Create server.js
Create a file named server.js and add:

const express = require("express");
const path = require("path");

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.use(express.static(path.join(__dirname, "public")));

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
    res.sendFile(
        path.join(__dirname, "public", "index.html")
    );
});

app.get("/products", (req, res) => {
    res.sendFile(
        path.join(__dirname, "public", "products.html")
    );
});

app.listen(PORT, "0.0.0.0", () => {
    console.log(`E-commerce app running on port ${PORT}`);
});

5. Create the HTML pages
Create a folder named public:
mkdir public


Inside it, create index.html:
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>E-commerce Home</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to Our Online Store</h1>
    <p>Find your favourite products here.</p>
    <a href="/products">View Products</a>
</body>
</html>

Now create products.html in the same folder:

<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Our Products</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Our Products</h1>
    <ul>
        <li>Laptop</li>
        <li>Mobile Phone</li>
        <li>Headphones</li>
    </ul>
    <a href="/">Back to Home</a>
</body>
</html>


ecommerce/
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
├── server.js
└── public/
    ├── index.html
    └── products.html

6. Create the Dockerfile
In the root ecommerce folder, create a file named exactly Dockerfile (without .txt).

Add:
FROM node:20-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY package.json ./
RUN npm install

COPY server.js ./
COPY public ./public

EXPOSE 3000

CMD ["npm", "start"]

7. Build the Docker image
Run this command from the folder containing the Dockerfile:
docker build -t shop_ecommerce .


Check that the image was created:
docker images


8. Run the Docker container
docker run -d --name shop_ecommerce_container -p 3000:3000 shop_ecommerce


Here:
- -d runs the container in the background.
- --name assigns a container name.
- -p 3000:3000 maps host port 3000 to container port 3000.
- shop_ecommerce is the image name.
9. Verify the application
Open these URLs in your browser:
Home page

http://localhost:3000/

Open

Products page

http://localhost:3000/products

Open

Check the running container:
docker ps


View its logs:
docker logs shop_ecommerce_container


10. Stop, restart, or remove the container
Stop it:
docker stop shop_ecommerce_container


Restart it:
docker start shop_ecommerce_container


Remove it when no longer needed:
docker rm -f shop_ecommerce_container


Removing the container does not remove the image.
